{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"Using tobacco is the most common cause of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"Using tobacco is the most common cause of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"The risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer is about 5 to 10 times higher for current smokers than for people who have never smoked.","source_span":"The risk of these cancers is about 5 to 10 times higher for current smokers than for people who have never smoked."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"For cigarette smokers, the risk of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer increases with the number of cigarettes smoked per day.","source_span":"the risk of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer increases with the number of cigarettes smoked per day"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"The risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer is about twice as high in people who have 3 to 4 alcoholic drinks per day compared with people who don't drink.","source_span":"about twice as high in people who have 3 to 4 alcoholic drinks per day...compared with those who don't drink alcohol"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"The risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer is 5 times higher in people who have 5 or more alcoholic drinks per day compared with people who don't drink.","source_span":"5 times higher in people who have 5 or more alcoholic drinks per day compared with those who don't drink alcohol"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"The risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer is 2 to 3 times higher in people who use both tobacco and alcohol than in people who use only tobacco or only alcohol.","source_span":"The risk of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer is 2 to 3 times higher in people who use both tobacco and alcohol than it is in people who use only tobacco or only alcohol."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"The risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer is about 35 times higher in people who smoke 2 or more packs of cigarettes per day and have more than 4 alcoholic drinks per day than in people who have never smoked or consumed alcohol.","source_span":"about 35 times higher in people who smoke 2 or more packs of cigarettes per day and have more than 4 alcoholic drinks per day than it is in people who have never smoked cigarettes or consumed alcohol"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"Betel quid contains areca nut, which is a cancer-causing substance.","source_span":"Betel quid contains areca nut, which is a cancer-causing substance."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"The risk for oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer is higher when chewing gutka than when chewing betel quid alone.","source_span":"The risk for oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer is higher when chewing gutka than when chewing betel quid alone."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"Betel quid and gutka chewing is common in many countries in South Asia and Southeast Asia, including China and India.","source_span":"Betel quid and gutka chewing is common in many countries in South Asia and Southeast Asia, including China and India."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"Being infected with certain types of HPV, especially HPV type 16, increases the risk of oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"Being infected with certain types of HPV, especially HPV type 16, increases the risk of oropharyngeal cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"The risk of oropharyngeal cancer is about 15 times higher in people who have oral HPV 16 infection compared with people who do not have it.","source_span":"The risk of oropharyngeal cancer is about 15 times higher in people who have oral HPV 16 infection compared with people who do not have oral HPV 16 infection."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"When people stop smoking cigarettes, their risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer decreases by one half (50%) within 5 years.","source_span":"their risk of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer decreases by one half (50%) within 5 years"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"Within 20 years of quitting smoking, a person's risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer is the same as for a person who never smoked cigarettes.","source_span":"Within 20 years of quitting, their risk of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer is the same as for a person who never smoked cigarettes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"A personal history of head and neck cancer increases the risk of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"A personal history of head and neck cancer increases the risk of oral cavity cancer and oropharyngeal cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","statement":"It has not been proven that stopping alcohol use will decrease the risk of oral cavity or oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"It has not been proven that stopping alcohol use will decrease the risk of oral cavity cancer or oropharyngeal cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How much higher is the risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer for current smokers compared to people who never smoked?","answer":"The risk is about 5 to 10 times higher for current smokers than for people who have never smoked."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How much higher is the risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer for someone who has 5 or more alcoholic drinks per day compared to a non-drinker?","answer":"The risk is about 5 times higher for people who have 5 or more alcoholic drinks per day compared with those who don't drink alcohol."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How much higher is the cancer risk for someone who smokes 2 or more packs a day and drinks more than 4 alcoholic drinks a day, compared to someone who never smoked or drank?","answer":"The risk is about 35 times higher for people who smoke 2 or more packs of cigarettes per day and have more than 4 alcoholic drinks per day than for people who never smoked or drank alcohol."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","fact_refs":[10,11],"question":"Which HPV type is linked to oropharyngeal cancer, and how much does it raise the risk?","answer":"HPV type 16 is especially linked to oropharyngeal cancer; oral HPV 16 infection raises the risk about 15 times compared with people who don't have the infection."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","fact_refs":[12,13],"question":"What happens to a smoker's risk of oral cavity and oropharyngeal cancer after they quit smoking?","answer":"The risk drops by half (50%) within 5 years of quitting, and after 20 years it becomes the same as for a person who never smoked."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_9","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What cancer-causing substance is found in betel quid?","answer":"Betel quid contains areca nut, which is a cancer-causing substance."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"The pancreas is a gland about 6 inches long that is shaped like a thin pear lying on its side.","source_span":"The pancreas is a gland about 6 inches long that is shaped like a thin pear lying on its side."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"About 95% of pancreatic cancers begin in exocrine cells.","source_span":"About 95% of pancreatic cancers begin in exocrine cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"Risk factors for pancreatic cancer include smoking, being very overweight, having a personal history of diabetes or chronic pancreatitis, and having a family history of pancreatic cancer or pancreatitis.","source_span":"Smoking.    - Being very overweight.    - Having a personal history of diabetes or chronic pancreatitis.    - Having a family history of pancreatic cancer or pancreatitis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"Hereditary conditions linked to pancreatic cancer risk include MEN1 syndrome, hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer (Lynch syndrome), von Hippel-Lindau syndrome, Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome, and FAMMM syndrome.","source_span":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome...Hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer (HNPCC; Lynch syndrome)...von Hippel-Lindau syndrome...Peutz-Jeghers syndrome...Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome...Familial atypical multiple mole melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of pancreatic cancer include jaundice, light-colored stools, dark urine, pain in the upper or middle abdomen and back, weight loss for no known reason, loss of appetite, and feeling very tired.","source_span":"Jaundice...Light-colored stools.    - Dark urine.    - Pain in the upper or middle abdomen and back.     - Weight loss for no known reason.     - Loss of appetite.    - Feeling very tired."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"Pancreatic cancer is difficult to detect and diagnose early because there aren't noticeable signs or symptoms in the early stages, its symptoms resemble those of many other illnesses, and the pancreas is hidden behind other organs.","source_span":"There arent any noticeable signs or symptoms in the early stages of pancreatic cancer...like the signs and symptoms of many other illnesses...The pancreas is hidden behind other organs such as the stomach, small intestine, liver, gallbladder, spleen, and bile ducts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"A tumor marker test for pancreatic cancer measures substances such as CA 19-9 and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA).","source_span":"A procedure in which a sample of blood, urine, or tissue is checked to measure the amounts of certain substances, such as CA 19-9, and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","statement":"In a PET scan, a small amount of radioactive glucose is injected into a vein, and malignant tumor cells show up brighter because they take up more glucose than normal cells.","source_span":"A small amount of radioactive glucose (sugar) is injected into a vein...Malignant tumor cells show up brighter in the picture because they are more active and take up more glucose than normal cells do."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How long is the pancreas and what shape is it described as having?","answer":"The pancreas is a gland about 6 inches long, shaped like a thin pear lying on its side."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What percentage of pancreatic cancers begin in exocrine cells?","answer":"About 95% of pancreatic cancers begin in exocrine cells."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are some of the main risk factors for pancreatic cancer?","answer":"Risk factors include smoking, being very overweight, a personal history of diabetes or chronic pancreatitis, and a family history of pancreatic cancer or pancreatitis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are common signs and symptoms of pancreatic cancer?","answer":"Common signs and symptoms include jaundice, light-colored stools, dark urine, pain in the upper or middle abdomen and back, unexplained weight loss, loss of appetite, and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why is pancreatic cancer difficult to detect and diagnose early?","answer":"There are no noticeable signs or symptoms in the early stages, the symptoms resemble those of many other illnesses, and the pancreas is hidden behind other organs like the stomach, liver, and spleen."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What tumor markers are measured in a tumor marker test for pancreatic cancer?","answer":"The test measures substances such as CA 19-9 and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors form in hormone-making cells (islet cells) of the pancreas.","source_span":"Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors form in hormone-making cells (islet cells) of the pancreas."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"Endocrine pancreas cells are also called islet cells or islets of Langerhans.","source_span":"Endocrine pancreas cells are also called islet cells or islets of Langerhans."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"Pancreatic NETs are much less common than pancreatic exocrine tumors and have a better prognosis.","source_span":"Pancreatic NETs are much less common than pancreatic exocrine tumors and have a better prognosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome is a risk factor for pancreatic NETs.","source_span":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome is a risk factor for pancreatic NETs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"A gastrinoma forms in cells that make gastrin, usually in the head of the pancreas and sometimes in the small intestine, and most gastrinomas are malignant.","source_span":"A gastrinoma usually forms in the head of the pancreas and sometimes forms in the small intestine. Most gastrinomas are malignant (cancer)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"When increased stomach acid, stomach ulcers, and diarrhea are caused by a gastrin-making tumor, it is called Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","source_span":"When increased stomach acid, stomach ulcers, and diarrhea are caused by a tumor that makes gastrin, it is called Zollinger-Ellison syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"An insulinoma forms in cells that make insulin, forms in the head, body, or tail of the pancreas, and is usually benign and slow-growing.","source_span":"Insulinomas are usually slow-growing tumors that rarely spread. An insulinoma forms in the head, body, or tail of the pancreas. Insulinomas are usually benign (not cancer)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"A glucagonoma forms in cells that make glucagon, usually forms in the tail of the pancreas, and most glucagonomas are malignant; too much glucagon causes hyperglycemia.","source_span":"A glucagonoma usually forms in the tail of the pancreas. Most glucagonomas are malignant (cancer)...Too much glucagon causes hyperglycemia (high blood sugar)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"VIPomas make vasoactive intestinal peptide and may also be called Verner-Morrison syndrome.","source_span":"VIPomas, which make vasoactive intestinal peptide. VIPoma may also be called Verner-Morrison syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"Somatostatinomas are tumors that make somatostatin.","source_span":"Somatostatinomas, which make somatostatin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","statement":"A higher than normal amount of chromogranin A together with normal amounts of hormones such as gastrin, insulin, and glucagon can be a sign of a non-functional pancreatic NET.","source_span":"A higher than normal amount of chromogranin A and normal amounts of hormones such as gastrin, insulin, and glucagon can be a sign of a non-functional pancreatic NET."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What syndrome is a known risk factor for pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors?","answer":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome is a risk factor for pancreatic NETs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Where does a gastrinoma usually form, and is it typically benign or malignant?","answer":"A gastrinoma usually forms in the head of the pancreas (and sometimes the small intestine), and most gastrinomas are malignant."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome?","answer":"It is the condition caused by a gastrin-making tumor that leads to increased stomach acid, stomach ulcers, and diarrhea."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Where do insulinomas typically form, and are they usually benign or malignant?","answer":"Insulinomas form in the head, body, or tail of the pancreas and are usually benign, slow-growing tumors that rarely spread."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What condition results from too much glucagon produced by a glucagonoma?","answer":"Too much glucagon causes hyperglycemia (high blood sugar)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000025_2","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What other name is VIPoma also known by?","answer":"VIPoma may also be called Verner-Morrison syndrome."}
